`polylines` there is compile-cnc-job.ts:69 `contours.map((contour) => contour.polyline)` fed from collectLayerContours, which accepts 'imported-svg', 'text', 'traced-image' and 'shape' identically (src/core/cnc/collect-cnc-contours.ts:32-38). Nothing anywhere in that chain inspects object.kind or object.traceMode.

Concrete input -> wrong output. Scene: a TextObject 'HOLE' rendered from Roboto-Regular at sizeMm 10 (the size used by the repo's own glyph fixture, src/core/cnc/profile-paths-text.test.ts:26), on a layer with cutType 'engrave' and the default lineArtContours (absent -> 'inner'), default tool em-3175 (3.175 mm). lineArtSelectionApplies('engrave') === true (line-art-contours.ts:41-48, pinned by its test at line 108). The 'O' glyph arrives as two closed rings: the bowl's outer contour and its counter, separated by the stem weight, roughly 0.6-0.9 mm at 10 mm type. isTightPair sees four gaps of ~0.6-0.9 mm, all <= 3.175, so line-art-contours.ts:66 drops the PARENT — the outer bowl. The emitted job engraves only the inside of the counter; the letter's outline is gone. Same for D, P, B, R, o, e, a, d, p, b, g, q. The operator entered no setting to cause this and gets no output that mentions it.

src/core/cnc/motion-polish.ts:79-83:
```
function wantsCounterClockwise(direction: CncCutDirection, cutType: CncCutType): boolean | null {
  if (cutType === 'profile-outside') return direction === 'climb';
  if (cutType === 'profile-inside' || cutType === 'pocket') return direction === 'conventional';
  return null;
}
```
So climb + profile-outside -> wantCcw = true -> the toolpath is forced counter-clockwise (motion-polish.ts:56-57), and climb + pocket/inside -> wantCcw = false -> clockwise.

Winding is measured in the MACHINE frame with Y up (src/core/geometry/polyline-orientation.ts:1-2, 'positive = counter-clockwise'), and contours reach the compiler through toMachineCoords (src/core/cnc/collect-cnc-contours.ts:1), so "CCW" here really is CCW in the top view.

Derivation: M3 = clockwise viewed from above (angular velocity -z). For a tool centre feeding in +X, the uncut chip thickness at tooth angle theta (measured from +X) is h = f_z * cos(theta), so cutting only happens on the leading (+X) half. With material on the LEFT of travel (+Y), the engaged arc is theta in [90-a, 90). A clockwise tooth sweeps theta downward, entering that arc at theta=90 where h=0 and leaving at theta=90-a where h is MAXIMUM -> enter thin, exit thick = CONVENTIONAL (up-milling). Climb therefore needs the material on the RIGHT of travel. Walking a part's exterior with the part on your right is CLOCKWISE; walking a hole with material on your right is COUNTER-CLOCKWISE. The code does the exact opposite in both cases.

Concrete failure: DEFAULT_CNC_LAYER_SETTINGS.cutDirection = 'climb' (src/core/scene/machine.ts:316). A default 20x20 mm square profile-outside cut compiles counter-clockwise — pinned by src/core/cnc/compile-cnc-climb-default.test.ts:63 `expect(isCounterClockwise(...)).toBe(true)`. That is conventional milling shipped under the label "Climb". Conversely, an operator on a belt-driven machine who picks "Conventional" in the dropdown (src/ui/layers/CncLayerToolFields.tsx:231) to avoid climb's self-feeding grab receives wantCcw = false for profile-outside = clockwise = REAL climb — the precise failure mode ADR-251 names as the risk it is protecting against ('climb's self-feeding force snapping an axis across backlash and grabbing').

The error is stated as a premise in three places: motion-polish.ts:7-11 ('with an M3 (top-view clockwise) spindle, CLIMB cutting keeps the material on the LEFT of travel'), machine.ts:55 (same claim), DECISIONS.md ADR-251 ('climb milling (outside-profile = counter-clockwise with an M3 / top-view-clockwise spindle)') and ADR-252 ('with an M3 spindle climb keeps the material on the LEFT of travel'). The sub-claim 'circling a part's exterior counter-clockwise puts the part on the left' is correct; the material-side premise is what is backwards.

